# 14. Branches & Variations

## MiniBatchKMeans

Uses small batches.

Good for:

```text
Large datasets
```

Advantages:

- Faster
- More memory efficient

---

## K-Medoids

Instead of using the mean as the center, it uses actual data points as representative centers.

Can be more robust to some outliers.

---

## Fuzzy C-Means

A point can belong partially to multiple clusters.

Example:

```text
Customer A:

Cluster 1 → 70%
Cluster 2 → 30%
```

---

## Gaussian Mixture Models (GMM)

Uses probabilistic clustering.

A data point can have probabilities of belonging to different clusters.

---

# 15. Edge Cases & Failure Modes 🚨

## ❌ Non-Spherical Clusters

K-Means generally works best when clusters are reasonably compact under its distance-based assumptions.

Example:

```text
🌙 Moon-shaped clusters
```

K-Means may struggle.

---

## ❌ Different Cluster Densities

Very dense and very sparse clusters can cause problems.

---

## ❌ Different Cluster Sizes

Large and small clusters may be difficult to partition appropriately.

---

## ❌ Outliers

Means are sensitive to extreme values.

One outlier can shift a centroid.

---

## ❌ Wrong K

Choosing an inappropriate number of clusters can create misleading groupings.

