# 22. Explain to an Interviewer — 60-Second Script 🎤

> K-Means is an unsupervised machine learning algorithm used to group similar data points into K clusters. It works by initializing K centroids, assigning each data point to its nearest centroid, and then recalculating each centroid as the mean of its assigned points. This process repeats until convergence. K-Means minimizes the within-cluster sum of squared distances, also called inertia. Important practical considerations include feature scaling, centroid initialization, and selecting an appropriate K using methods such as the Elbow Method and Silhouette Score. Its main limitations are sensitivity to outliers and difficulty handling non-spherical or highly uneven clusters.

